####################################################################
# cat2a_process_priority.dlog — HAND-AUTHORED. NOT GENERATED.
#
# NOT LOADED AS A STANDING RULE — engine/replay_driver.py's build_script
# no longer `import`s this file (see its own ONDEMAND_RULE_NAMES comment).
# The join below is instead run as a one-shot `select ... limit 1` query,
# hand-translated into engine/replay_driver.py's ONDEMAND_QUERY_BODIES["cat2a"]
# (RDFox's bracket quad syntax used throughout this file is rule-only —
# confirmed directly it errors inside a plain `select` command — so the
# query version is a real, separately-maintained SPARQL rewrite, not this
# file re-used verbatim). THIS FILE REMAINS THE CANONICAL DEFINITION of
# the join/filter logic — replay_driver.py's copy must be kept in sync BY
# HAND with any change made here, verified via the same cat2a_pos/cat2a_neg
# regression fixtures that validate this file.
#
# One rule per file (split out of the former cat_rules.dlog) so
# poc_entry.py can enable/disable each CAT rule independently. RDFox Datalog
# port of CODE/evaluation_poc/reasoner/rules/cat2a_process_priority.rq,
# plus an arrival-order fix the original translation was missing — see
# the migration plan for full background.
#
# REAL BUG FOUND AND FIXED, NOT JUST A SYNTAX CHECK: this rule originally
# derived `silencedBy` in BOTH directions for any two mutually-qualifying
# alarms (confirmed via an equal-priority fixture returning 2 answers
# instead of 1), because the original SPARQL's pre-bound `?alarm`
# (assess.py's initBindings, fixing it to specifically the ARRIVING alarm)
# enforced an arrival-order guarantee invisibly that a continuously-
# materializing Datalog rule does not get for free. Fixed with explicit
# `mda:hasStart` comparisons.
#
# The category guard (`hasCategory alarmcat:Physiological` on BOTH
# alarms) is preserved exactly — closes a real bug in the original SPARQL
# (a Technical alarm borrowing clinical content from a shared Signal
# particular), confirmed still blocked under RDFox's own graph-scoped
# join semantics via the category-guard regression fixture.
#
# mda:priorityRank and mda:isPropertyOf are left UNGRAPHED deliberately:
# both are STANDING, concept-level facts (priority_rank.ttl,
# inference.ttl), loaded once into the default graph, never touched by
# the window operator — not per-alarm content. An earlier version of this
# rule scoped priorityRank to the alarm's own transient graph, matching a
# bug in the (now-deleted) hand-authored data/archetypes.py that re-minted
# it per occurrence; reverted once that bug was found.
#
# mda:silencedBy (domain mda:Alarm, range mda:Alarm, the only new
# predicate this file introduces) has exactly one consumer: the per-alarm
# `silencedBy` check in build_script, run once right when that alarm
# arrives — nothing else ever reads it back. That's WHY this rule is no
# longer loaded as a standing rule at all (see the NOT LOADED note above):
# a fact with one point-in-time reader doesn't need RDFox continuously,
# incrementally re-maintaining it against every unrelated transaction in
# the store for the rest of the process's life. (History: it was briefly
# left permanently ungraphed — "faithful port of the original CSV-row
# semantics" — then briefly scoped to ?g1/the alarm's own transient graph
# once that permanence was found to be the likely cause of a progressive
# incremental-materialization slowdown that timed a real run out on a
# 6272-alarm patient. Both are moot now that the rule isn't materialized
# at all — this file's ?g1 graph terms below are vestigial from that
# scoped version, harmless to leave since they document which graph each
# atom's own content actually lives in, which the on-demand SPARQL
# translation still needs to know.)
#
# SAME-PATIENT CONSTRAINT — REQUIRED, NOT OPTIONAL, ONCE PATIENTS SHARE
# ONE DSTORE. mda:approximates/mda:isPropertyOf's shared pivot (?process)
# is a STANDING, CONCEPT-level node (e.g. physiologicalProcess:
# CardiacContraction) — legitimately the SAME node across every patient
# who has a HeartRate-derived metric, not a per-patient particular. Under
# the original one-dstore-per-patient design this was harmless (nothing
# from a DIFFERENT patient's dstore could ever be visible to this join at
# all); confirmed as a REAL bug the moment patients started sharing one
# dstore (engine/replay_driver.py's build_script): two different real
# corpus patients, each with their own tachycardia/bradycardia-style
# alarm active at an overlapping real-world instant, derived silencedBy
# ACROSS patients. `?msg`/`?activeMsg` share `?patient` below to close
# this — the same fix cat2b_metric_sensor.dlog needs for the identical
# reason.
####################################################################

@prefix mda:              <https://w3id.org/mda/ontology#> .
@prefix alarmcat:         <https://w3id.org/mda/vocab/alarm-category/> .

[?alarm, mda:silencedBy, ?active] ?g1 :-
    [?alarm, mda:hasCategory, alarmcat:Physiological] ?g1,
    [?alarm, mda:hasMessage, ?msg] ?g1,
    [?msg, mda:concernsPatient, ?patient] ?g1,
    [?msg, mda:triggeredBy, ?device] ?g2,
    [?device, mda:hasSensor, ?sensor] ?g3,
    [?sensor, mda:sensorProducesSignal, ?signal] ?g4,
    [?signal, mda:analyzedBy, ?analysis] ?g5,
    [?analysis, mda:producesMetric, ?metric] ?g6,
    [?metric, mda:approximates, ?property] ?g7,
    [?property, mda:isPropertyOf, ?process],
    [?alarm, mda:hasPriority, ?incomingPrio] ?g1,
    [?incomingPrio, mda:priorityRank, ?incomingRank],
    [?alarm, mda:hasStart, ?alarmStart] ?g1,

    [?active, mda:hasCategory, alarmcat:Physiological] ?g8,
    [?active, mda:hasMessage, ?activeMsg] ?g8,
    [?activeMsg, mda:concernsPatient, ?patient] ?g8,
    [?activeMsg, mda:triggeredBy, ?activeDevice] ?g9,
    [?activeDevice, mda:hasSensor, ?activeSensor] ?g10,
    [?activeSensor, mda:sensorProducesSignal, ?activeSignal] ?g11,
    [?activeSignal, mda:analyzedBy, ?activeAnalysis] ?g12,
    [?activeAnalysis, mda:producesMetric, ?activeMetric] ?g13,
    [?activeMetric, mda:approximates, ?activeProperty] ?g14,
    [?activeProperty, mda:isPropertyOf, ?process],
    [?active, mda:hasPriority, ?activePrio] ?g8,
    [?activePrio, mda:priorityRank, ?activeRank],
    [?active, mda:hasStart, ?activeStart] ?g8,

    FILTER(?active != ?alarm),
    FILTER(?activeRank >= ?incomingRank),
    FILTER(?activeStart < ?alarmStart) .

# VALIDATION — executed against real RDFox: positive (unequal priority,
# arrival-order respected) → 1. Equal-priority variant → 1 (only after the
# arrival-order fix). Category-guard regression (Technical alarm sharing a
# Signal/Metric chain with a Physiological one, via a SEPARATE persistent
# graph) → 0. Re-validated end-to-end via engine/replay_driver.py's
# cat2a_pos/cat2a_neg fabricated cases — cat2a_pos required
# mint.py's clinical_context (mda:approximates is an OWL-RL entailment
# off inference.ttl's bridge axioms, not a directly-minted fact; see
# mint.py's own docstring).
